try this: Just like you did for dates, you need to short-circuit the “time-by-number” case *before* your free-form parsing kicks in. In your `sendMessage`, move the time-selection branch up into that early `if (numMatch)` block. For example:

```diff
   const sendMessage = async (messageText = null) => {
     const textToSend = messageText || input;
+    // grab the fresh step value
+    const currentStep = ctx.step;

     // … your existing setup …

     // detect a pure number
     const numMatch = textToSend.trim().match(/^[0-9]+$/);
     if (numMatch) {
       const num = parseInt(numMatch[0], 10);

+      // ——— TIME SELECTION BY NUMBER ———
+      if (currentStep === 5 && availableTimes.length >= num && num > 0) {
+        setInput("");
+        // pick the nth time from your array
+        handleTimeSelect(availableTimes[num - 1]);
+        return;
+      }

       // ——— DATE SELECTION BY NUMBER ———
       if (currentStep === 4 && availableDates.length >= num && num > 0) {
         setInput("");
         handleDateSelect(availableDates[num - 1]);
         return;
       }

       // ——— SERVICE & WORKER cases… ———
       if (currentStep === 2 && commonServices.length >= num && num > 0) { /* … */ }
       if (currentStep === 3 && workers.length >= num && num > 0)       { /* … */ }
     }

     // … rest of your sendMessage …
   }
```

### What this does

1. **Reads `ctx.step` freshly** so you’re never stuck on an old value.
2. **Catches “step 5” numbers first**, before you fall back into any date-or-text parsing.
3. **Calls** your existing `handleTimeSelect(...)` with the right index.

Now typing `1` at the time-prompt will call `handleTimeSelect(availableTimes[0])`, typing `2` → `availableTimes[1]`, and so on—exactly like your date list.

